    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The most common culprits for build failures during automated testing are often unit test failures, integration issues, or environment configuration drift. In staging, the sticking points usually involve performance bottlenecks or subtle integration bugs that only appear with realistic data loads. To prevent slippage, you must monitor test coverage religiously, and ensure your staging environment mirrors production as closely as possible. Failing to address these issues early is what leads to production surprises, which is exactly what the gate is meant to stop.",
      "source": "target",
